WEBVTT

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.620
Thunderbirds, fly with the best!

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:27.480
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.460
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:29.600
I'm Sean.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.700
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:42.980
Thunderbirds, the TV show from the 60s was filmed in Super Mario Nation or Thunderbirds on the NES in Super Mario Nation.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.680
Eh? That's what I thought you said.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.320
Yeah, right? It's a puppet show.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:58.800
I guess they were doing VistaVision or other kinds of things and they were pretending like they invented this new thing to record puppets.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.800
And they called it, and I'm going to probably say it the same way again, but they called it Super Mario Nation.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.380
Yeah, it's like a...

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:07.920
Yeah, a marionette.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.740
Don't say that word.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.080
That's like a bad word.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:13.500
A marionette?

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.660
Yeah, you said it again.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.200
What are you doing? Oh, you want to hear about puppet.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.520
Puppet. Yeah. Puppet.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.120
So, I had no idea what this even was.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.060
Had you guys heard of Thunderbirds before?

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.520
And the whole time I was playing it, I was like, I think this is based on a TV show.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.800
And that might be because you can kind of tell, but also I was like, that sounds familiar.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:45.000
It wasn't until this very moment where I had the same existential horror I'm sure we all had by looking up the original show and seeing what it actually looks like.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.140
And it's pretty terrifying.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.540
My thing was that I kept seeing...

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:59.680
So, we'll get into this, but between each mission in the game, you get these little cut scenes with the characters.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.920
And I was like, why do they look like that?

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.960
And they all just have this weird doll look.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.800
And then it's like, oh, they just do look like that.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:10.460
They are puppets.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.340
It was strange.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.280
Now, we all watched an episode of the show.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.060
And for those of you who don't know, it was a British TV show.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:28.300
And I feel like the marionettes thing and the puppet nature of it all is like what was impressive about it.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.760
Because it was kind of a...

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.740
I mean, it's British TV, so it was always going to be dry.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.340
But it's really dry.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.420
It's not necessarily funny.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.460
It's not that action packed.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:42.280
So, it was like, was the cell that it's just really impressive that we can do this shit with puppets?

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.700
Yeah, is it taking itself pretty seriously then?

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.300
I think so. I would say that, yeah, it was like, wow, this is actually small.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.960
I think that it's the same thing with claymation, but we're a bit more exposed to claymation.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:12.920
Whereas I think, at least for myself, I can say that my only real exposure to something like this was Team America World Police, which I now have to imagine is basing 90% of itself off of the show.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.340
Right, right. Yeah, I got that vibe as well.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:26.640
And I can't think of too many other, like, I'm sure there are plenty of others, but like, this isn't just like, other than the Muppets, like, I didn't really expose myself to like puppets on TV.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.720
Yeah, yeah, Sesame Street, Muppets, things like that.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.420
Yeah. What about, what's the, Fraggle Rock?

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.180
I never watched it.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.360
No, yeah, but that's all, that's all like the Henson Company stuff.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:43.380
Yeah, right, right. I guess I just mean like, I'm sure that there's other stuff out there besides Thunderbirds and Team America.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.480
Like, there's probably a large gap of this stuff that we're missing.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.540
But that was my knowledge going into this.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:58.240
And it's funny because Thunderbirds was a British TV show in the 60s, and I'm sure it came over to America.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.380
I'm sure like at some point it ran in syndication or something.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:09.660
But like, this is another one of those weird games where it's like, not getting a revival at this very moment in 1990.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:16.120
But like, they're like, yeah, we'll make a game about the Thunderbirds and a TV show from long ago.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.740
And they do nothing to like, enforce that on the box art.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:27.760
Not, there's a ship on the box art, but none of, that ship is not from the Thunderbirds.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.500
It's not one of their five ships.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.120
That's the whole thing, right? Thunderbirds five.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.460
Like, there's five of them.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.640
I think there's five of them. And this is now the sixth ship.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.800
Like, we haven't seen this.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:44.580
Like, I was, it was funny to see that they did try again.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.580
And with the, you know, the, the superstar Bill Paxton had a movie.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.280
Oh, yeah. The live action movie.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.480
Yeah. Which I think is very strange that they would just cut the whole concept.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.320
Yeah, they just, they should have at least had those people on strings, right?

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.760
Like, it should have been like wires you could see, but they went for like that effect.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:14.560
It's kind of like the reverse of the, the Wachowski brothers, Speed Racer movie, which was like hyper aware of its

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:22.960
itself and it's like, and the tropes it was playing into, but also incredibly stylized and, you know, cinematically beautiful.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:33.600
I feel like Thunderbirds didn't commit to anything about the puppetry or like, no, but also just like, it didn't commit to like poking fun at the original source either though.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.400
Right? Like it kind of, it kind of like, why did you believe that stuff was awesome?

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:45.320
2004 though, is like sort of in the middle of this, like, it's sort of like a dearth of culture and all movies are kind of terrible though.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:51.860
You know? Well, Speed Racer was 2008 and there are people who will say that was terrible, but they are objectively wrong.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:56.960
You can tell anybody who says that 2008 Speed Racer movie wasn't good, that they're bad.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:58.140
Not essential.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.740
I'm just going to stay out of that one because I haven't seen it, but I'd probably dislike it.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.120
Great. Yeah. You would love watching that.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.260
Watch that for sure.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.660
I think you'll enjoy it. Anyway, Thunderbirds, the video game.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:10.140
Let's, let's get into it.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:11.260
The video game. Yeah.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:17.320
The video game. There are like, there's like a couple of different ways I could imagine this going.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.520
I guess I just didn't imagine shmup.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.200
Um, and, and it seems silly.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:30.400
I know. But like the Thunderbirds are like a team of five that like kind of work together to get

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.800
something done. And this tries to do that of like, oh, this time you're playing as this vehicle or this

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.100
time you're, you know, you're this guy and you, you get to choose like where you want

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.620
to go so you can like take things out of order.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.220
Sure. But ultimately at the end of the day, you're just picking which level of the shmup you want

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:49.700
to play. Yeah.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.860
And, uh, there is at first like a little bit of appeal to the idea like, yeah, you're going

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:55.860
to be in the submarine.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.520
You're going to be in the underground drill or something.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.480
You're going to be on this Jeep or in a plane, but yeah, they're, they're not, I, I,

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.620
I'm impressed with the, uh, how much the, it looks different, but they don't really play

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:13.100
very different. I mean, it's, it technically plays different, but it doesn't feel different.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:23.680
What they did here is that they took every power up from like Gradius and just like sort

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.620
of separated them all so that you couldn't get them all at once or you couldn't choose

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:30.620
which one you had.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.900
It's just, Oh, this, this ship gets this upgrade.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:40.980
And like, so that's why I think like, yeah, there's like some that made them all feel different.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:48.900
That's just because they kind of, they just split apart any other shmups power system and

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.360
scattered them a bunch between a bunch of different characters.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.120
Yeah. For, for my money.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.660
I mean, I, I know you were being nice, Joe, but like the, whether you're on, whether you're

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:03.640
in the air, in the sea or on land, they, it is like identical gameplay.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.880
Yeah. I mean, I guess I, I caught myself while I was making that point because I'm watching

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.220
and like, I am realizing like, yeah, I guess it's technically not.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.620
I mean, there literally are different power ups that do slightly different things.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.060
They just don't feel that different.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.380
Right. You know, there's a, but like the end of the day, it's still like, you're not like in

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.440
the, when you're on land, you're not like free roaming or, or racing.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.020
You're just still doing the thing where you just shoot a bunch of enemies on the screen

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.200
as the auto scrolls.

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:33.480
But where it's like the, the submarine thing, you get like a different projectile.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.080
And then like when you're in the, I don't know, you're in like a, some underground

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:43.140
thing and you get like a giant long laser beam looking flame where it's like, yeah, that,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.900
that presents very minimal differences.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.720
But like, until I really thought about it, it never felt very different to me.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:55.860
I'd say that the only, the, the actual space in which this game is sort of setting itself

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.540
apart from other shmups aside from the tie in or whatever, and the level structure.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:08.020
Sure. Sure. Um, would be that in the moment to moment gameplay, they've sort of centered

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.820
the whole option, uh, aircraft thing.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.560
Whereas like in, you know, other shmups you get options, but they're just one of any number

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.680
of other upgrades.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.620
Whereas this gets its own slot and you kind of collect different orientations of them.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.460
And that's, that's something.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:32.800
That's a good start of a concept, you know, like, yeah.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.880
And I, I, I could see that really paying off.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.520
I don't know if, uh, if it did.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:43.680
Yeah. It's funny. Cause I actually think like the actual gameplay and this is going to sound

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.520
crazy cause we played so many of these, but like, I really couldn't play this game cause

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.460
I couldn't stand to look at the screen.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:56.400
There's something about like the way that the screen is scrolling and the way that all the

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.960
graphics looked at it actually hurt my eyes to play this game.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.920
Oh yeah. I remember the moment.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.200
It's like a strobing effect or something.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:13.200
It's pretty early in the game, uh, that you go to some, uh, I guess some active volcano.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:19.620
You know, um, on the planet where you, yeah, it's just screaming red at your face.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:25.520
Like, cause you get into some like molten lava sea and then not only are you shooting at,

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:32.120
uh, red enemies, but you're also being shot with red projectiles at other times.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:37.820
And it's just the, one of the most unlegible moments in this podcast.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.580
I think. Yeah.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.780
I really couldn't believe it because it was so hard for me.

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:43.860
I had to keep playing.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:47.180
Obviously I think that's technically that could be the second level you encounter.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:52.900
Um, if you do the first mission and then go to that mission next, but like, wow.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.740
Like it was so hard for me to like, look at that level.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:58.180
Like I was like, am I getting older?

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:04.860
What's happened? I had, I, I, I had like a similar thought, but maybe, maybe for different reasons.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.080
I do feel like I didn't feel like this game was hard to look at.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:15.820
I felt like it, it, it, it almost reminded me of like 1942 in the sense where it's like,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:20.160
there's, it's like 1942 and the way it moves, but then there's a lot going on.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:23.300
That's like, it feels disconnected from my gameplay.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.860
Like, whereas like a lot of other shmups I, I do with a lot of shmups actually like find them.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:36.660
Sometimes like hard to look at mostly with like the shmups that I think are really good because there's so much happening and so much flashiness.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:51.520
But also it's like, I'm, that makes it feel like fast and like intense, but that lava level, like that's hard to look at because of things that are happening that like doesn't, it doesn't translate to like, I'm moving really fast and I'm dodging things.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:53.360
And then, oh my God, all this stuff's happening to me.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.860
It's just like, yeah, I just can't see the, I just can't see the ships because they're the same color as the lava.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:02.380
Yeah. Like it's, it's just not, I don't know.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:09.100
I, I guess it, I, it didn't immediately sound to me like, wow, this is hard to look at because most shmups for me are kind of like hard to look at sometimes.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:14.320
But it, to me, it was just like, why does this feel like I should, everything should be happening faster?

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:30.340
The game just sort of has this, this ongoing problem with, again, legibility, like being able to play the game because you might have your next level be this one of the, like with that underground one.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.040
And you will just sort of blow up.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:40.300
You'll, you'll go forward and then you'll just kind of blow up and you'll wonder, why did I just blow up there?

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:44.040
There was no, there were no projectiles coming for me.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.020
I didn't hit a wall.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:55.300
And then you realize that, um, they have a very interesting way of communicating that there are breakable blocks in front of you that just kind of look like background noise.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:01.280
Um, but no, that's actually something you've got to dig through and give yourself carpal tunnel shooting through.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:07.260
Um, where, where, like, I really have no issue with that as a concept in a level.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:14.820
I, it's just that the way that they designed how it looked makes it look like something you can't interact with.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:16.280
Yeah, it looks like the ground.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:21.480
I had this, this was very frustrating to me, uh, this exact moment you're talking about.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:29.520
Cause there's these light brown walls and then there's like dark brown floors that you don't interact with.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.940
And then there's like a, a medium dark brown.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.020
That's like very close to the regular brown.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:35.620
Stalactites and stalagmites maybe.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:45.540
Yeah. And like, that's like you blow up, but it's all, it's like, and it's also, it's not only is it like just a slightly different color, it's like kind of the same texture too.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.420
Yeah. It's this weird textured thing.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:55.000
They don't look like, they don't look like, uh, there's like a, a clear distinction to like where it starts and where it stops.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.780
It's just this weird, it's just this weird fuzz on the screen.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:00.260
That's like, Oh, if you run into that, you blow up.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.560
And sometimes it's so vague where I'm like, I didn't even realize it was there.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:20.980
Now the part of the game I thought you were going to talk about, Sean, is like a differentiator is that, yes, you have this mission select screen where you can kind of choose which levels you want to go into, but you also have a overall 60 day challenge to defeat the game.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:30.580
If you don't get to the end of the game within 60 days of the game time, um, then, uh, the villain who's referred to as the hood.

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:35.900
I'm not sure if he wears a hood, if he's from the hood, uh, not, not sure what that's about.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.800
Didn't, didn't look into it, but the concept of the hood, right?

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.320
The hood will win.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:45.120
Uh, if you don't, um, get to the end by the 60 days.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:52.420
And obviously that means that every time you take on a mission, it counts as a day and you can fail those missions.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:15:05.520
So, uh, you do have kind of a way to lose, um, just by not being able to complete the game in X amount of time, which, uh, you know, I think is, is an interesting idea.

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:12.940
That just by making it like, Oh, every time you try a mission, a day has passed is not like really capitalizing on that idea.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:23.280
It should have brought some strategy into the overall map where you have to like, kind of not waste time by going to places that won't help you with the mission.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:25.040
If that makes sense. Yeah.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:33.880
I think I, I, I definitely get it and I appreciated it as something like sort of like a meta game.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:45.940
Um, my, my main exposure to something like this would be like XCOM and that's done very well, but I've also played a lot of XCOM and know how that works.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.400
And I haven't really played too much of Thunderbirds.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:16:01.600
So maybe if I played the full game, if I'm, if I did that 60 days, um, and failed and succeeded, maybe it does do something for the balance.

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:09.340
I appreciate that there's something that you're thinking about between mindlessly shooting forward and sometimes to the left and right.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:14.260
Uh, so I, I, I like it as an idea on paper at the very least.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:15.160
Yeah. Same.

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:25.020
The part that intrigued me about it is this fact that like when you die, like instead of lives or continues, when you die, you just lose a few days.

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:36.040
And I think that that is a cool way to contextualize like your, your experience where it's like, you can keep going as long as you want until you run out of these days.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:37.360
It's not a binary.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:38.480
You failed.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:40.240
Start the game. Yeah.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:41.460
It's like you, you failed.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:44.200
It's going to take you some time to get back on your feet.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:45.780
So like two more days are lost.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.600
Plus these days are ticking by.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:56.740
Um, I, I think, and now this is something, I don't know, maybe the game does do this and I just didn't play enough of it to like really, uh, get this.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:05.240
But I think what I need a little more out of it is like, I need to know when I'm at the point of no return, you know, when am I wasting my time?

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:09.860
Because like, there is something kind of cool about like, Hey, if you're good enough.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:18.700
And even though you've lost all these days, like, Hey, now it's, it's just getting harder and harder, but now we got to like really lock it in and not lose another, another run.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:21.280
You know, like something cool about that.

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:24.360
